SRC/strsch -- one change made for this collection=================================================bmgtest.c and bmgtest2.c (2026-09-18): UNIX FILE MODES ARE NOT OS-9 MODES.Both opened a named file with `open(name, 0)'.  On Unix that is O_RDONLY;on OS-9 mode 0 means NO ACCESS BITS AT ALL, so the open succeeds and everyread through the descriptor is refused.  What that looked like:    $ bmgtest beta f.txt        (nothing at all)    $ bmgtest beta < f.txt    beta-- a search that finds nothing in a file it can plainly see, and finds itat once on standard input.  Measured 2026-09-18.Both files now define BMG_RDONLY -- S_IREAD under OSK, 0 elsewhere -- anduse it.  Nothing else is touched.THIS IS A CLASS, NOT AN INCIDENT.  `patch' had the same fault in five places(SRC/patch/README.OSK), and it was found there first by the os9exec session,which traced `I$Open D0.b=$00' followed by `I$Read ... E_BMODE'.  A scan ofevery .c file under disk/SRC on 2026-09-18 found 76 non-comment call sitesof `open(x, 0)' or `creat(x, 0NNN)' across 34 source trees.  Most areharmless and some are deliberate:  * a stat() shim opens with mode 0 ON PURPOSE -- a GetStat needs no access    bits, and SRC/diff/os9.c says so in its own comment, "can't even open it    for NO access".  blarslib, smail, lua, eff_make and infoxpress all do    this and are right.  * several sites are inside `#ifdef MACOS' or `#ifdef VMS' and are never    compiled here -- unzip50/file_io.c is one.  * SRC/world/helper.c already has `#ifdef OSK ... open(Q1TEXT, 1)', so    somebody met this before and fixed that one.WHAT THE REST OF THE LIST CAME TO, checked 2026-09-18.  Of the seven sitesleft after patch and these two, NONE is a live defect in a shipped program:  dvips/file_p.c:38      followed by _gs_gfd -- a stat, needs no access  wish/files.c:166       the same, with an S_IFDIR fallback  eff_bench/system.c:67  opens and closes at once: an existence test  canfield/cfscores.c    ALREADY fixed, with its own OS-9 note; the scan                         matched the words of that note, not code  adv/okplay.c:61        reads, but okplay.c is not in `advent's recipe --                         it is in no shipped binary  cnews/appendfile.c:23  reads, and would be broken -- but no `appendfile'                         ships; the source is here and the binary is not  trek73/save.c:215      reads, and is UNREACHABLE: the OSK port disables                         saving outright ("Saving a game is not possible on                         OS-9", save.c:90), because a save writes the whole                         data segment out and restores it with brk().  So                         no save file can exist to restore.  Measured by                         playing it: code 31 prints that line.That last one was fixed and then REVERTED.  The fix was right and changednothing a reader can reach, and a rebuilt binary that behaves identically ischurn on a program the collection took as it found it.So the class is closed OVER disk/SRC: two live defects (patch, andbmgtest/bmgtest2), and everything else deliberate, dead code, or alreadydone.AND THAT IS THE LIMIT OF IT, which 2026-09-19 made plain.  The scan readsSOURCE, so it can only close the class for programs whose source is here --and `texidx' is a shipped binary with no source anywhere, carrying exactlythis bug.  Traced by the os9exec session on a clone of this image:`I$Open D0.b=$00 "sample.idx"', then `I$Read' answering E_BMODE, and a readloop that takes the refusal for "buffer too small" and QUADRUPLES thebuffer -- F$SRqMem $2010, $8010, $20010, $80010 and on -- until the requestpasses the arena.  So it ends at "virtual memory exhausted" whatever memoryit is given; measured here across five arena sizes on a three-line .idxfile, and the request tracked the arena exactly.It cannot be patched safely either.  The three I$Open sites in the binaryare all inside the C library's own open() wrapper, taking the mode from thecaller in d1, so the byte to change is wherever the CALLER puts zero there-- which is not findable with confidence in 24 KB of stripped code.What this means for the class: it is closed for source we have, and OPENfor the 298 programs that have none.  `texidx' is the one instance anybodyhas found, and it was found by running the program and reading the numbers,not by scanning.  That is the only method left for the rest.